# nanopb size constraints for moon_companion.proto
#
# Every message must fit in a single GATT notification (~244 bytes on a
# 247-byte MTU link). We don't currently do multi-notification
# reassembly, so the caps here matter — if the encoded envelope exceeds
# 244 bytes, moon_ble_send rejects it and the RPC fails.

# ── Envelopes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*MoonRequest.auth_token       max_size:16
*PairRequest.flipper_name     max_size:32
*PairResponse.auth_token      max_size:16
*PairResponse.phone_name      max_size:32

# ── Position ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*PositionData.source_id       max_size:8

# ── Time ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*TimeData.tz_name             max_size:48

# ── Notifications ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*SendNotificationRequest.title  max_size:64
*SendNotificationRequest.body   max_size:160
*SendNotificationRequest.tag    max_size:32

# ── HTTP proxy ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keep caps tight — every HttpRequest/HttpResponse envelope must clear a
# single GATT notification. Typical requests fit easily; a worst-case
# request with 8 headers + 512 B body + 128 B URL + auth_token + envelope
# is already uncomfortably close to 244 B.
*HttpRequest.method           max_size:8
*HttpRequest.url              max_size:128
*HttpRequest.headers          max_count:4
*HttpRequest.body             max_size:256
*HttpHeader.key               max_size:32
*HttpHeader.value             max_size:96
*HttpResponse.headers         max_count:4
*HttpResponse.body            max_size:150
